
DAY 8 – LOGS & MONITORING

1) What are Logs?
Logs are text files that record system activities, errors, user actions,
and application events. Logs help administrators understand what happened,
when it happened, and why it happened.

2) Importance of Logs in DevOps
- Helps in debugging issues
- Tracks system and application errors
- Useful for security auditing
- Helps in troubleshooting production failures

3) Common Log Locations in Linux
/var/log/syslog  - General system logs
/var/log/auth.log - Authentication and login logs
/var/log/dmesg - Kernel and boot logs

4) Log Viewing Commands
cat /var/log/syslog        - View entire log file
less /var/log/syslog       - View logs page by page
tail /var/log/syslog       - View last lines of log file
tail -f /var/log/syslog    - Live log monitoring

5) What is Monitoring?
Monitoring is the process of continuously checking system resources
like CPU, memory, disk usage, and services to detect problems early.

6) Monitoring Commands
top     - Shows CPU and memory usage
df -h   - Shows disk usage in human-readable format
du -sh * - Shows directory size
systemctl status service_name - Checks service status

7) Conclusion
Logs help in identifying problems after they occur.
Monitoring helps in preventing problems before they occur.
Both are very important in DevOps.

